  <details class="more">
    <summary><span class="chev"></span><span class="label-closed">Read the full description</span><span class="label-open">Hide description</span></summary>
    <div class="more-body">
      <p>Downfall is a four stage 3D obstacle course built in Godot, closer in spirit to Fall Guys than to a standard benchmark. All four stages have to be cleared in a single episode, and the agent gets no hard coded path.</p>
      <p>The policy sees a deliberately thin observation vector: normalized raycast distances in five directions with a type code for ground, wall or hazard, a vector to the current stage goal, a one hot stage index, an on ground flag and vertical velocity. It outputs continuous movement, rotation, and a jump value thresholded into a discrete jump. Reward is shaped from progress toward the goal, a small per step time penalty so standing still is never optimal, a large bonus at each stage gate, and a penalty for falling below the safe height or being hit by a hazard.</p>
      <p>PPO from Stable Baselines3 trains across eight parallel Godot instances at five times game speed for a million environment steps, logged to TensorBoard. All reward logic lives in the Godot scripts and is exposed to Python through a small bridge, so changing the incentive structure never touches the training code. That separation is what made iterating on the reward model faster than iterating on the policy.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
